00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
elgato has been positioning themselves  as the kind of company that could sell  you  everything you possibly need for your  at-home streaming setup  i'm talking capture cards microphones  lighting  and now acoustic sound paneling that's  right the  stuff you put on your walls when you  want to improve the sound quality of  your room  these are the elgato wave panels not to  be mixed up with the wave  which is confusingly also the name of  their brand of microphone  the starter pack will run you 100 us  dollars for  six panels which is a solid chunk of  change especially since you can go to  sites like  amazon and buy cheaper foam panels in  bulk elgato sent us a starter pack to  try out  so in this video we're going to break  down what you get in the box  how easy installation is whether they  actually make a difference to your sound  quality  and if they are worth that 100 us  dollars  and remember if you enjoy this video  please hit the like and  sub button if you haven't already it  means a lot to us  so your first question might be why  would you want these anyway  well the layout of your space plays a  huge role in the quality of your  sound in short audio can reverberate off  walls and hard surfaces  this affects the sound coming out of  your speakers as well as  audio recordings filling your room with  soft objects such as  couches carpets and curtains can help  absorb  sound but there are still plenty of  places with  hard surfaces that need to be covered  mainly  your walls and ceilings and that is  where acoustic  paneling comes in if your room is  properly treated it can result in  dramatically improved acoustics  so what actually comes with this starter  pack well in the box are six  hexagonal foam panels they are 19 inches  in width running from  point to point the panels are actually  made from  two different materials a low density  foam that is for dampening the high  range frequencies  and a high density fiber behind that for  trapping the mids and lows  the size of the panels are quite nice  and while i'm no expert when it comes to  the world of  foam they are of a nicer quality than  the cheaper  less dense stuff that i've worked  previously with in studios  but what makes this more of a premium  product to me is the  mounting frames which is by far the most  appealing part for me  acoustic foam can be a right pain in the  ass  to set up there are wall friendly  solutions like command strips but those  don't really stick to foam  and then you got things like staple guns  or glues which is  fine in a studio environment but it is  absolutely going to tear up your walls  at home and  you definitely do not want that there  are  wall friendly solutions you can come up  with to mount that kind of cheap foam  but it is going to require more time and  resources on your part  elgato solution is a plastic frame which  you just set up on your wall and then  you mount the foam on  and that's it it is a quick easy clean  solution  and it gives you everything you need out  of the box to make it happen  and to elgato's credit the setup process  was a lot smoother and faster than i  figured it would be  the only construction really required is  to snap the frame pieces into place  and then you're ready to go the next  step being planning out where to put  your panels  this is where elgato falls short  ideally you really want to plan out  where to put your  acoustic paneling to best fit your space  but elgato doesn't provide any resources  or  tips either in their instructions or  their website  the best you get is a couple of designs  that they offer you to try out  okay but which one's gonna give me the  best sound so you're gonna need to do  some extra research  on your own to figure out where the best  position is to fit your personal setup  speaking of i live in a small  one-bedroom apartment and my computer  setup is  in my living room which is by no means  the  optimal situation for this kind of setup  but  i'm just working with what i have which  i imagine is the case  for most people so for my situation i've  decided the best course of action is to  set  up all my panels right behind my monitor  and speakers which  should help dampen my voice now when it  comes to installation  you have two options sticky adhesive  for smooth walls and screws for rougher  walls with the recommendation of using  both for extra stability now my walls  are pretty rough  but i am not really that interested in  putting holes in them  so i ended up using the sticky adhesive  and it worked out pretty  well just remember to wipe down your  wall with a mix of water and  isopropyl alcohol first now once you get  your first frame set up it's as simple  as using the sides to line up the rest  and then  joining them all with the connecting  piece you then place the foam panels on  and you're pretty much done a painless  setup in my opinion  and pretty easy to take down and move  somewhere else if needed  the sticky adhesive that comes with is  pretty much exactly like the command  strips you can find  in any store if you need replacements  now comes the big question do they  actually make a difference  for this i've recorded two simple audio  tests before and after  hello this is me testing my microphone  before we set up the elgato  wave panels this is me testing my  microphone  after we've set up the elgato wave  panels  i'll admit that at first i didn't really  feel like there was much of a difference  when i was just going about my daily  routine recording vo for work  but now that i've compared them side by  side it's actually a pretty noticeable  change  thanks to the panels my audio is softer  and  less sharp which is more pleasing on the  ears  you can see this when comparing the  waveform of my  audio the peaks in the audio where i set  up the wave panels is a lot  shorter because there isn't any reverb  like i said i was originally skeptical  of the actual improvements these wave  panels could make to my audio  but having now listened to it i'm  pleasantly surprised and happy with the  results  the question now is whether it's worth  that 100 dollar  price tag you will undoubtedly be saving  money buying the cheaper stuff  in bulk but the quality of that foam  isn't gonna be on par with the wave  panels  there's also the matter of setup i  cannot express  just how much of a pain it is to set up  that cheaper foam paneling and how much  damage it can potentially do to your  walls  the easy installation is a huge plus for  me  and yes they are a little expensive but  once you start to compare them to the  professional grade acoustic paneling  which ranges in the  hundreds of dollars they start to look a  lot more reasonable  you're also paying for the presentation  and appearance  of professionalism the sleek hexagonal  design  doesn't look ugly on your walls and  appears well on camera on a set  i won't deny that after i first set them  up i did feel more professional during  my voiceover i was originally ready to  write these off as a  unnecessarily expensive version of the  cheaper foam you can get on amazon  however the quality of the foam is  actually really nice and the ease of  installation  makes it a pretty easy recommendation  for me especially if you're interested  in improving the sound quality  of your at-home streaming setup hey  everybody thank you for watching this  video hopefully it's giving you some  good information  if you are interested in checking out  the elgato wave panels  or you've just now learned about sound  paneling in general and  want to check that out for yourself  regardless of what foam  you end up going with again as i  mentioned at the beginning of the video  if you haven't already please give us a  like and sub  it means a lot to us it tells us uh that  these videos are doing well and that you  guys like them and that you want to see  more like these such as previous videos  that i've done  such as ones where i compared two fifty  dollar headsets one from razer and one  from hyperx so why not check that out  thanks for watching  [Music]  you

